lesson g1-making-ten v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=2 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Making ten"
  goal "Find the number that makes ten with any number from one to nine, and use it to add past ten."

  look {
    scene 34x20 {
      tenframe frame count=7 color=berry at=canvas(1, 1)
      equation eq "7 + {?more} = 10" right-of=frame gap=1
      row both below=frame gap=1 space=2 align=end {
        undercup cup shown=7 cups=1 hidden=3 lifted=true total=10
        tree t fruit=7 fallen=3 item=apple
      }
    }
    say """
      Count the counters, then count the empty squares. Together they make ten. Lift the cup and
      the three are there. Seven apples on the tree and three on the ground is ten apples too.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice bonds.make-ten count=1 hard-count=0 seed=3
    practice bonds.under-the-cup count=2 seed=4
    level easy {
      worked add.bridge-ten a=8 b=5
    }
    practice add.bridge-ten count=2 seed=3
    practice add.two-frames count=1 easy-count=2 seed=4
    practice add.missing-past-ten count=1 easy-count=0 hard-count=2 seed=9
  }
  story {
    show story.apples-to-ten n=6
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show add.balance-sides a=7 b=5 c=8 easy-a=6 easy-b=4 easy-c=7 hard-a=9 hard-b=7 hard-c=8
  }
  try stars=3 {
    level easy medium {
      show bonds.three-cups t=10 easy-t=9
    }
    level hard {
      show bonds.two-cups-all-ways t=13
    }
  }
  remember {
    say "Ten is a friendly number. To add past it, fill the ten first: 8 + 5 is 10 and 3."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The first question can be answered by counting empty squares, and that is fine as a way in. The
    cups take the squares away, so the pair has to be known. Filling the first frame is the strategy
    the grade is aiming at: eight and five becomes ten and three. The two frames show the same move
    with counters, and the missing part asks for it the other way round: 8 + ? = 13. The level scale
    can be done by working out both sides, but ask whether there is a quicker way (eight is one more
    than seven, so its partner is one less than five). If every question still needs counting from
    one, stay here another day.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the first number is eight or nine, so one or two counters move across, a worked example comes before the adding, and the missing-part question is left for another day."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level three or four counters move across, the totals reach eighteen, and the last question asks for every pair that makes a total. A child who starts at nine and works down will not miss one."
  }
}
